1. Auto Scaling Group (ASG) – Theory
      Definition
                  An Auto Scaling Group (ASG) in AWS is a service that automatically launches or terminates EC2 instances based on scaling policies to maintain desired capacity and ensure application availability.
      Key Components of ASG
                  Launch Template / Launch Configuration
                  Defines how new EC2 instances will be created:
                  AMI ID (Windows Server in our case)
                  Instance type
                  Security Group
                  Key Pair
                  Storage configuration
    
@ Minimum, Maximum, Desired Capacity
                 * Min → Lowest number of instances always running.
                 * Max → Upper limit to control costs.
                 * Desired → Number of instances ASG tries to maintain.
                 * 
@ Scaling Policies
                 * Manual Scaling → User sets desired capacity manually.
                 * Dynamic Scaling → Automatically adds/removes instances based on CloudWatch metrics (e.g., CPU > 70% → scale out).
                 * Scheduled Scaling → Scale at specific times (e.g., more instances during office hours).

@ Health Checks
                 * EC2 Health Check → Checks if instance is running.
                 * ELB Health Check → Used when integrated with a Load Balancer — ensures only healthy instances serve traffic.

@ Termination Policies
                 * Decide which instance to terminate first (e.g., oldest, closest to next billing hour).
@ Benefits of ASG
                 * High Availability: Ensures a fixed minimum number of healthy instances.
                 * Cost Optimization: Scales down when demand is low.
                 * Fault Tolerance: Automatically replaces unhealthy instances.


2. Application Load Balancer (ALB) – Theory
          Definition
                 * An Application Load Balancer is an L7 (HTTP/HTTPS) load balancer that distributes incoming application traffic across multiple EC2 instances in one or more AZs.

# Key Features
                 * Layer 7 (Application Layer)
                 * Can inspect HTTP headers, URL paths, hostnames.
                 * Supports path-based & host-based routing.
@ Target Groups

                 * Collection of registered targets (EC2 instances).
                 * Health checks are performed at target group level.
@ Listeners
                 * Check for incoming connections (e.g., Port 80 or 443).
                 * Rules forward requests to target groups.

@ Cross-Zone Load Balancing
                 * Distributes traffic evenly across all instances in all AZs.
                 * Sticky Sessions

Keeps the same user connected to the same instance during a session.

Benefits of ALB
                 * Distributes traffic evenly for better performance.
                 * Supports SSL/TLS termination for security.
                 * Improved fault tolerance by removing unhealthy instances.

3. ASG + ALB Integration (Windows Instances)
When integrated:
                 * ALB’s target group is linked to the ASG → new instances automatically get registered.
                 * ASG health checks use ALB health checks for better reliability.
                 * Traffic is automatically spread to new Windows instances when scaling occurs.
                 * If an instance fails, ASG launches a new one and ALB routes traffic to the healthy ones.

Workflow
                 * User request → ALB Listener (Port 80)
                 * ALB applies routing rules → sends to Target Group
                 * Target Group contains ASG-managed Windows EC2 instances
                 * ASG adds/removes instances based on scaling policies
                 * ALB continuously checks health and stops sending traffic to unhealthy ones

4. Real-World Use Case
Example:
                 * An E-commerce website running on Windows Server IIS:
                 * During sales → ASG scales from 2 to 10 instances.
                 * ALB ensures all users get routed to the least busy healthy instance.
                 * After sale → ASG scales down to 2 to save cost.

5. Interview-Level Points
                 * ASG + ALB is a stateless architecture — session data should be stored in external storage (S3, DynamoDB, RDS).
                 * Use Elastic IP only with bastion hosts; ALB handles traffic distribution for application servers.
                 * Always spread instances across multiple AZs for HA.
                 * Prefer target tracking scaling for consistent performance.



@ Why This is Important in Real World
        High Availability: If an EC2 fails, ASG launches a new one automatically
        Scalability: Increases/decreases EC2 count based on CPU/traffic
        Cost Efficiency: You only pay for what you use
        Disaster Recovery: Keeps minimum required servers always running


